(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-quiz"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does it usually mean when a patient develops symptoms from left bundle branch block?","choices":["Severe heart disease","A drug side effect only","A benign normal variant","An accessory pathway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most patients are asymptomatic; those who develop symptoms usually have severe underlying cardiac disease and so do not tolerate the block well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does an implantable cardioverter defibrillator do?","choices":["Detects fatal rhythms and shocks","Paces only during bradycardia","Records the rhythm only","Ablates an accessory pathway"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The implantable cardioverter defibrillator prevents sudden cardiac death: sensing electrodes detect fatal arrhythmias and the device delivers a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which are the main causes of frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Hypertension and diabetes","Dehydration and fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hyperthyroidism and anemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) are the main causes, so new frequent premature ventricular contractions call for checking oxygen and electrolytes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Once stabilized after unstable ventricular tachycardia, what do most patients need?","choices":["Lifelong oral magnesium","No further treatment","An implantable defibrillator","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once stabilized, most patients need an implantable cardioverter defibrillator for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death from ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Compared with Mobitz type I, Mobitz type II block carries a higher risk of which outcome?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Mobitz type I block","Complete heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II has a higher risk of progression to complete (third-degree) heart block and sudden cardiac death, which is why it is treated even without symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which physiologic state is a named cause of Mobitz type I block?","choices":["Increased vagal tone","High sympathetic tone","Hyperthyroidism","Mild dehydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased vagal tone from an overreactive vagus nerve is listed first among the causes of Mobitz type I, with nodal blocking drugs, Lyme carditis, ischemia and ablation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"After transcutaneous pacing stabilizes a patient in third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block, which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Wearable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Emergent transcutaneous pacing bridges the patient, and the definitive treatment for complete heart block is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is polymorphic ventricular tachycardia treated, even when the patient has a pulse?","choices":["Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous lidocaine alone","Observation on a monitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even if the patient looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does the prognosis of left bundle branch block depend on?","choices":["The patient's atrial rate","Serum magnesium level","The underlying heart disease","The height of the R wave"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis depends on the type and severity of the underlying heart disease and on whether other conduction disturbances are present. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"For an elderly patient with Mobitz type II block found incidentally and causing no symptoms, which plan is appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance only","Recheck in a year","Start a beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms because of its unpredictable progression to complete block, and the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which PR interval pattern identifies Mobitz type I block?","choices":["Fixed PR with random dropped QRS","Fixed PR longer than 0.20 seconds","P waves unrelated to the QRS","PR lengthens until a QRS drops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) block the PR interval progressively increases from normal to long until a QRS complex is dropped, then the cycle repeats. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"For premature ventricular contractions under 1% of beats with no structural heart disease and no symptoms, which is the plan?","choices":["Reassure and remove triggers","Lifelong beta blocker","Catheter ablation","Implantable defibrillator"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-risk patients with a low burden (under 1% or 1,000 a day), no structural heart disease and no symptoms need reassurance and elimination of triggers. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What decides how severe the symptoms of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block are?","choices":["Serum potassium alone","The patient's age alone","Escape rate, site, heart disease","Atrial rate and P wave shape"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms range from none to severe and depend on the ventricular escape rate, the site of the escape rhythm and the presence of underlying heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which two arrest rhythms are shockable?","choices":["Tachycardia, pulseless electrical","Fibrillation, pulseless tachycardia","Asystole and fibrillation","Pulseless electrical, asystole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia are treated with defibrillation; asystole and pulseless electrical activity are unshockable. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes within what time of collapse?","choices":["Thirty days","One hour","Ten minutes","Six hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden cardiac death is generally defined as unexpected natural death from cardiac causes occurring within 1 hour of the patient's collapse. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which is the management of asymptomatic first-degree atrioventricular block?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","No treatment needed","Permanent pacemaker","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No treatment is needed unless the patient has debilitating symptoms or heart failure symptoms; the vast majority of first-degree blocks cause none. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which history places a patient with premature ventricular contractions in the high-risk group?","choices":["Daily caffeine use","Age under 30","Occasional anxiety","Prior syncope"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-risk features are a burden over 15%, premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, pre-existing structural or conduction disease and a history of syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which acute event can cause right bundle branch block by suddenly raising right ventricular pressure?","choices":["Aortic dissection","Hypertensive crisis","Acute gastroenteritis","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which arrest rhythms carry the best prognosis?","choices":["Asystole only","Ventricular tachycardia, fibrillation","Asystole, pulseless electrical","Pulseless electrical activity only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among cardiac arrest rhythms, ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation have the best prognosis; asystole and pulseless electrical activity are poor. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which parasitic infection is a named cause of third-degree (complete) atrioventricular block?","choices":["Giardiasis","Chagas disease","Malaria","Toxoplasmosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chagas disease is listed among the causes of complete block, with Lyme carditis, bacterial endocarditis with an abscess and inflammatory or infiltrative disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drug is given repeatedly during cardiac arrest from ventricular fibrillation, alongside shocks and cardiopulmonary resuscitation?","choices":["Adenosine","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine","Dopamine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine is given by intravenous or intraosseous push at intervals throughout the arrest, alongside defibrillation and high-quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"For hemodynamically stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, which approach is used?","choices":["Intravenous antiarrhythmic","Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stable, monomorphic sustained ventricular tachycardia is pharmacologically cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide, after a monitor, intravenous access, a 12-lead electrocardiogram, oxygen and labs. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"In an elderly patient, which named cause of right bundle branch block reflects aging of the conduction system?","choices":["Sudden pulmonary embolism","Viral myocarditis","Accessory pathway","Age-related degeneration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Age-related degenerative change of the conduction system heads the list of causes of right bundle branch block, as it does for first-degree block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a pulse is treated with what?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In unstable monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, check for a pulse: with a pulse, synchronized cardioversion; without one, defibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which chronic pulmonary vascular condition is a named cause of right bundle branch block?","choices":["Pleural effusion","Obstructive sleep apnea","Chronic sinusitis","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is a named cause of right bundle branch block, along with age-related degeneration, ischemic heart disease, cardiomyopathies and inflammatory or infiltrative disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which treatment is used for symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Catheter ablation","Biventricular pacemaker","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, except symptomatic block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which is treated with a biventricular pacer. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the two leading causes of pulseless electrical activity in adults?","choices":["Hyperkalemia and acidosis","Tamponade and pneumothorax","Hypothermia and toxins","Hypovolemia and hypoxia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The two leading causes of pulseless electrical activity in adults are hypovolemia and hypoxia; the heart has organized electrical activity but nothing to pump or no oxygen. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic is given for ventricular fibrillation that persists after shocks?","choices":["Amiodarone","Adenosine","Diltiazem","Digoxin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia persists after shocks, amiodarone is given by rapid intravenous or intraosseous push. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Besides shocking, what can newer implantable cardioverter defibrillators also do?","choices":["Deliver antiarrhythmics","Ablate scar tissue","Act as pacemakers","Dissolve coronary clots"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Newer implantable defibrillators can also act as pacemakers, in addition to sensing fatal arrhythmias and delivering a shock. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"}]};
